How Did Jackie Robinson Die?
In his later years, Robinson faced increasing health problems. He suffered from type 2 diabetes complications for years. It caused him to lose sight in one eye and much of his vision in the other. The former baseball star also experienced pain and burning sensations in his legs from diabetic nerve and artery damage.
Robinson also had abnormally high blood pressure. In 1968, he had his first of three heart attacks. He eventually required regular cardiology care for his congestive heart failure.
The third heart attack ultimately caused Robinson’s death. Early on the morning of October 24, 1972, Robinson collapsed in his Stamford, Connecticut, home. He died shortly after at age 53.
Robinson’s funeral was held at the Riverside Church in New York City on October 27. Author Kostya Kennedy wrote about the service in his 2022 book True: The Four Seasons of Jackie Robinson. Although the church’s official capacity was only 2,500 people, thousands more either squeezed into its doors or gathered along nearby streets to honor the baseball hero.
Among the pallbearers carrying Robinson’s casket were NBA legend Bill Russell, as well as former teammates and peers like Pee Wee Reese, Larry Doby, and Ralph Branca. Other sports greats such as boxer Joe Louis and MLB stars Hank Greenberg, Hank Aaron, Ernie Banks, and Willie Stargell attended.
Reverend Jesse Jackson gave Robinson’s formal eulogy. “[Robinson] didn’t integrate baseball for himself,” Jackson said. “He infiltrated baseball for all of us, seeking and looking for more oxygen for Black survival, and looking for new possibility.”
Robinson is buried next to his son Jackie Jr. at Cypress Hills Cemetery, six miles from the site of his former home ballpark Ebbets Field.
Legacy: Jackie Robinson Day, Museum, and More
Getty Images
Jackie Robinson’s No. 42 adorned the pitching mound of Fenway Park in Boston during a 2020 game.
Since his death, Robinson’s life has been memorialized in many ways. A New York City park in Harlem bears his name, and his jersey has been retired throughout the MLB. He also has a dedicated museum and an annual MLB honorary event, Jackie Robinson Day.
There are multiple monuments honoring Robinson, including a statue located outside Dodger Stadium depicting him stealing home plate.
No. 42 Jersey Retirement
The Dodgers were the first to retire Robinson’s jersey number of 42. They did so in 1972, the same year he died.
Decades later, on the 50th anniversary of Robinson’s historic debut—April 15, 1997—MLB executive Bud Selig retired No. 42 from the major leagues entirely. Hall of Fame New York Yankees pitcher Mariano Rivera was the last player to wear the number full-time, as players who had it at the time of Selig’s decree were allowed to keep it until retirement.
